│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ staggered_rows_gauss_seidel                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Two improvements:                                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Layout**: The previous layout [4,5,4,5,4,4] had a flaw — the last two rows were both │
│                          │ 4-circle rows with the *same* x-positions (no hex stagger), forcing the vertical spacing  │
│                          │ dy to be at least 0.2, which pushed the whole grid out of the walls and caused cascading  │
│                          │ radius shrinkage (sum only 1.59). New design: rows [4,5,4,5,4,4] where the top 4-row is   │
│                          │ *staggered* by half a spacing (x = 0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7), so every adjacent row pair has       │
│                          │ horizontal offset 0.1. Then every nearest-neighbor distance is sqrt(dy² + 0.1²), and we   │
│                          │ solve the fixed point r = sqrt(((1-2r)/5)² + 0.01)/2 ≈ 0.0954, placing the bottom/top     │
│                          │ rows exactly at distance r from the walls. This makes nearly all 26 circles touch their   │
│                          │ neighbors and the walls, giving sum ≈ 26 × 0.0954 ≈ 2.48.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Radius solver**: Proportional pairwise shrinking is suboptimal (it over-shrinks both │
│                          │ circles of a violating pair, causing cascades). Replace with a Gauss–Seidel pass: r_i =   │
│                          │ min(wall_i, min_j (d_ij − r_j)), iterated to convergence. This is still guaranteed        │
│                          │ feasible (each radius respects the current neighbor radii and walls) but assigns radii    │
│                          │ much more generously, and it naturally handles the asymmetric wall constraints at the     │
│                          │ boundary.                                                                                 │
