│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lp_radii_plus_hill_climb                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ Key insight: with circle centers fixed, maximizing the sum of radii is a *linear          │
│                          │ program*: maximize Σr_i subject to r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij (pairwise) and r_i ≤ min border       │
│                          │ distance. The previous uniform-radius assignment (r = min over all constraints) massively │
│                          │ underuses space — border and corner circles can be much larger than lattice circles. So   │
│                          │ I: (1) keep the hexagonal lattice as a starting layout, (2) solve the exact LP for        │
│                          │ optimal radii at those positions (scipy `linprog` with HiGHS — fast for 26 variables /    │
│                          │ ~350 constraints), and (3) run a hill-climb over centers: random jitter perturbations     │
│                          │ with a decreasing step size, re-solving the LP after each move and accepting              │
│                          │ improvements. Wall-constrained circles also get directed pushes away from walls. A tiny   │
│                          │ safety epsilon (1e-9) on all constraints plus a final 1-1e-6 radius shrink guarantees     │
│                          │ strict feasibility. A greedy fallback keeps the program working if scipy is unavailable.  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
