╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ warm_started_shift_search                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current layout (4,5,4,5,4,4 rows) is already strong (2.47 vs target 2.635), but the   │
│                          │ fixed hex lattice is not the exact optimum: the solver only computes maximal radii        │
│                          │ *given* the centers, and the seed positions leave slack (e.g., the top row of 4 at        │
│                          │ x=0.1..0.7 leaves the top-right corner region underused, and tiny perturbations of        │
│                          │ centers change how radii can be redistributed). The known remaining gap (~0.16) comes     │
│                          │ from center positions, not the radius solve. I add a coordinate-shift local search: for   │
│                          │ each circle, try shifting it by ±delta in x and y at several delta scales, re-solve       │
│                          │ radii, and keep improvements. The key efficiency insight (per the recommendation) is      │
│                          │ warm-starting the Gauss-Seidel solver from the previous radius vector — after a           │
│                          │ 0.001-scale shift, radii converge in a handful of passes, so each candidate evaluation is │
│                          │ cheap and we can afford 3 delta scales and multiple sweeps well within the time budget    │
│                          │ (the fast solver currently converges in <0.15s, and warm-started re-solves take only a    │
│                          │ few passes). I also fix the top row to be pulled toward the right wall (x =               │
│                          │ 0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8-style but staggered 0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9) so corner slack is shared more       │
│                          │ evenly — actually I keep the seed and let the shift search discover improvements, only    │
│                          │ nudging the top row to span the full width (0.1..0.9 spacing would be 4 circles at        │
│                          │ 0.15,0.45,... no — I shift the last row to [0.1,0.3,0.5,0.9]... safer: let shift search   │
│                          │ handle it; only change row to [0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9][:4]→ keep). To stay safe, I only add: │
│                          │ (1) an `init` warm-start parameter to solve_radii, (2) a `_local_shift_search` function,  │
│                          │ and (3) call it in construct_packing. This is a strictly-improving hill climb: it only    │
│                          │ accepts center moves that raise the sum of radii, so validity and the current 2.47 are    │
│                          │ guaranteed as a floor.                                                                    │
