│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ corner_anchored_growth                                                                    │
│ patch_description        │ The current hill-climb pushes every wall-touching circle away from the wall it touches.   │
│                          │ That is correct for interior-edge circles, but wrong for corner circles: a circle         │
│                          │ touching two walls (e.g., r ≈ x ≈ y) has three-sided slack and gains radius by sliding    │
│                          │ INTO the corner, since only the diagonal neighbor constraint binds. The recommendation    │
│                          │ explicitly identifies asymmetric corner growth as the path toward the ~0.15 remaining     │
│                          │ headroom. This edit adds corner detection to the directed-move step: circles touching two │
│                          │ perpendicular walls are pushed diagonally into the corner, while single-wall circles are  │
│                          │ pushed away as before. To keep the eval time within budget while the greedy growth pass   │
│                          │ (which is the most reliable improver) still runs, the random hill-climb iterations are    │
│                          │ modestly reduced. This should let the LP assign large radii to the four corner circles    │
│                          │ (three walls + one neighbor), a hallmark of the best-known n=26 packings, raising         │
│                          │ sum_radii beyond 2.39.                                                                    │
