│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ corner_anchored_asymmetric_seed                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The best prior variant (2.39) used a uniform hexagonal seed, which forces all 26 circles  │
│                          │ into near-equal-sized lattice roles. Expert knowledge from circle packing says the        │
│                          │ optimal n=26 configuration gains most of its advantage from a few LARGE corner circles    │
│                          │ (three-sided wall slack) plus smaller filler circles — corner circles can have radius     │
│                          │ ~0.09-0.12 while interior lattice circles stay near 0.07-0.08. A symmetric lattice seed   │
│                          │ cannot discover this because the hill-climb's wall-push moves actively push circles AWAY  │
│                          │ from walls, preventing corner anchoring.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Changes:                                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. Modify the seed: pull the 4 lattice circles nearest each corner diagonally INTO the    │
│                          │ corners (asymmetric anchoring), giving the LP water-filling solver immediate three-sided  │
│                          │ slack there so it assigns them large radii.                                               │
│                          │ 2. Add directed corner moves in the hill-climb: circles touching two walls get pulled     │
│                          │ further into the corner (instead of being pushed away), while single-wall circles are     │
│                          │ still pushed away from the wall.                                                          │
│                          │ 3. Keep the final greedy growth pass unchanged — it now operates on a much better basin.  │
