│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_exact_lp_final_radii                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The water-filling radius solver (`compute_max_radii`) is only a heuristic approximation   │
│                          │ of the optimal radii at fixed centers: it maximizes each circle greedily and then shrinks │
│                          │ pairs to restore feasibility, which systematically loses total radius sum relative to the │
│                          │ exact solution. For fixed centers, the maximum-sum radii problem is a linear program      │
│                          │ (maximize sum r_i subject to r_i + r_j <= d_ij and r_i <= wall distances), solvable       │
│                          │ exactly with scipy's `linprog` (HiGHS) in well under a second for n=26 (only 26 variables │
│                          │ and ~350 constraints). A previous program that used this LP scored well; here we combine  │
│                          │ it with the much better center-position search already present. The key edit: after       │
│                          │ `_local_shift_search` finishes and the best centers are fixed, replace the heuristic      │
│                          │ `_greedy_enlarge` radii with the exact LP solution (falling back to the heuristic if      │
│                          │ scipy is unavailable or the LP fails), and keep a tiny safety shrink to guarantee strict  │
│                          │ validity. This should recover the radius sum lost to the heuristic repair step (typically │
│                          │ 1-3% of total, i.e. ~0.03-0.08 in sum), pushing sum_radii from ~2.49 toward ~2.55+, while │
│                          │ validity remains 1.0 and eval_time grows only marginally (one LP solve ≈ 0.01s).          │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0232                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
